exports[`git.branch.cutover given: [case21] a human asks for help when: [t0] begin --help then: it teaches the flags, with copy-paste examples 1`] = `
"usage: git.branch.cutover begin --from <@main|@tree|branch> --into <branch> [--pick <sha>]... [--mode plan|apply]

move this tree's work onto a fresh branch: the commits you pick,
and the uncommitted edits. the source branch stays as it was

options:
  --from @main      cut from fresh origin/main (else origin/master), after a fetch
  --from <branch>   cut from fresh origin/<branch>, after a fetch
  --from @tree      stack on this tree's HEAD; every commit is kept
  --into <branch>   the new branch to cut over into (must not exist)
  --pick <sha>      a commit to replay onto the new branch; repeat, in order
  --mode plan       preview the cutover (default)
  --mode apply      execute the cutover

examples:
  rhx git.branch.cutover begin --from @main --into turtle/wave-report-v2 --pick a1b2c3d
  rhx git.branch.cutover begin --from @tree --into turtle/reef-shoals --mode apply
"
`;

exports[`git.branch.cutover given: [case21] a human asks for help when: [t1] continue --help and abort --help then: each says what it does, with an example 1`] = `
"usage: git.branch.cutover continue

settle the conflicted files, stage them via rhx git.stage.add,
then continue to land the rest of the cutover. continue also
resumes a cutover a kill or a failed step interrupted

example:
  rhx git.stage.add src/band.ts
  rhx git.branch.cutover continue

usage: git.branch.cutover abort

undo the whole cutover: back to the source branch, edits restored,
the new branch removed

example:
  rhx git.branch.cutover abort
"
`;

